 German philosopher and polymath whose work spanned the 17th and 18th centuries. He is renowned for developing monad theory, proposing that the universe is composed of simple, immaterial substances called monads that reflect the entire cosmos from their own perspective. He also advanced a model of perception based on unconscious differential relations among microperceptions, suggesting that perceptual experience arises from internal discriminations rather than passive reception of stimuli. In the discussed text, Leibniz is invoked in contrast to Berkeley to explore the status of the perceived and the existence of bodies, highlighting debates about realism versus idealism and the nature of external objects. Additionally, Leibniz is noted as having substituted the notion of “metaschematism” for metempsychosis, indicating his engagement with theories of embodiment and identity. He is described as a thinker who analyzes the ideas of a field of individuation, enveloping and enveloped intensities, and the way these intensities relate to individuality and differences. Together, these descriptions present Leibniz as a thinker who weaves metaphysical, epistemological, and cognitive concerns into a coherent framework: monadic architecture underpins a dynamic, relational account of perception; his considerations offer an alternative to solely empiricist or sensationalist accounts of perception and body; and his exploration of intensities and individuation addresses how individuality arises from differences within a structured reality. In sum, Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz is portrayed as a foundational figure whose theories of perception, identity, and the nature of bodies reflect a unified program to reconcile unity and multiplicity through a richly articulated metaphysical system.", "FECHNER:Fechner, Gustav Fechner, was a German psychologist, physicist, and philosopher. He is renowned for his foundational role in psychophysics, a field that directly links conscious perception to