scourse.", "BLANCHOT:Maurice Blanchot is a French writer and philosopher who is presented as a major figure in the text, cited alongside Martin Heidegger as a great author who has explored the relation between the question and repetition. The text also references Blanchot as describing two figures—the personal death and the present death—in a philosophical discussion of metaphysical and existential themes. It identifies the other thinker as having gone farthest into “the Event” and characterizes this figure as a philosopher who explores the Event; in the context of the referenced material, this other thinker is Martin Heidegger. Additionally, the passage indicates that Blanchot is quoted in the text, with the quotation attributed to Blanchot’s own words.", "HUSSELR:Edmund Husserl, a German philosopher and founder of phenomenology (late 19th–early 20th century), is described in the text as revisiting Leibniz and developing a theory of appurtenance (appurtenance) and aperceptive transposition that ties the body to the self and perception.", "YVON BELAVAL:Yvon Belaval is a scholar who, along with Christiane Fremont, has shown that the vinculum is a 'reflecting wall' and an outside form dependent on the dominant or constant in Leibnizian relational theory.", "CHRISTIANE FREMONT:Christiane Fremont is a scholar who, along with Yvon Belaval, has shown that the vinculum is a 'reflecting wall' and an outside form dependent on the dominant or constant in Leibnizian relational theory.", "CARAVAGGIO:CARAVAGGIO is a Baroque-era painter referenced in the text as an example of Baroque art, used to illustrate the ubiquity of organisms within Baroque conceptions of matter and organicism."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_61c16b9898236c255ae32f3088a5275ee6c75cb4f491600e043359b27da2b784_v2"}